            "bug_id": 19276,
            "text": ".Random.seed is an ordinary variable that users, packages and restored workspaces can set to anything, so GetRNGkind() and FixupSeeds() in src/main/RNG.c check it and warn and reseed on invalid values. Three corrupt values get through the checks and crash R instead:\n\n- .Random.seed[2] negative with the Mersenne-Twister: FixupSeeds() tests `I1 <= 0` on an unsigned value, so negatives pass, and MT_genrand() indexes the state array with the negative number.\n- length(.Random.seed) == 0: GetRNGkind() reads element 0 before checking the length, and GetRNGstate() copies 625 ints from past the end.\n- .Random.seed[1] encoding the user-supplied normal kind (e.g. 10303) with no generator loaded: only USER_UNIF is checked, so rnorm() calls a NULL function pointer.\n\nA fourth, non-crashing case is fixed alongside: for L'Ecuyer-CMRG the all-zero flag is not reset between the two components, so an all-zero second component is accepted.\n\nReproducible example: attached as random-seed-validation-repro.R; each block segfaults, expected a warning and a fresh seed as for other invalid values.\n\nProposed patch: attached as random-seed-validation.diff, against r90825. It adds the checks at the existing choke point with the current warn-and-reseed behaviour, with a regression test in tests/reg-tests-1e.R. Valid states are unaffected: RNG output digests over all kinds, seeds and parallel streams are identical to unpatched trunk.\n\nThe patch is also submitted as https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/446, where CI results can be seen.",
